\f0\fs32 \cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	7	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 After that, you start implementation with a second prompt\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\cf0 After you review the generated specs/backlog, you tell the AI:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f1\fs34 \cf0 Start implementation of the first ready backlog item.\
\
Follow AI_PROJECT_BOOTSTRAP.md, CURRENT_STATE.md, AI_HANDOFF.md,\
IMPLEMENTATION_PLAN.md, BRANCH_AND_WORKTREE_GUIDE.md, TEST_PLAN.md,\
TRACEABILITY_MATRIX.md, and the linked spec.\
\
Use TDD where practical.\
Create the branch recommended in CURRENT_STATE.md.\
Do not work directly on main.\
Update all required state, traceability, test result, and handoff files before stopping.\
Prepare the PR review package when done.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f0\fs32 \cf0 So the full usage model is:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f1\fs34 \cf0 One-time:\
Big original prompt \uc0\u8594  creates reusable bootstrap scaffold.\
\
Every new project:\
Create empty folder\
\uc0\u8594  copy bootstrap scaffold files into it\
\uc0\u8594  paste new-project initialization prompt\
\uc0\u8594  AI fills scaffold for this specific project\
\uc0\u8594  you review specs/backlog\
\uc0\u8594  then ask AI to implement first ready item\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f0\fs32 \cf0 The only thing I would improve: keep the bootstrap output in one clean master folder, for example:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f1\fs34 \cf0 AI_BOOTSTRAP_MASTER/\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f0\fs32 \cf0 Then every time you start a new project, copy from that master folder into the new project.\
Do not keep editing the master every time unless you improve the reusable system itself. Otherwise, project-specific details may leak back into your reusable template.\
}